  "slug": "correlations-dynamics-111a",
  "summary": "New preprints use channel-level exceptional points, ensemble transitions and Gaussian ergotropy to probe correlations under dissipation and measurement.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how correlation structure and non-Hermitian spectral features govern open, monitored or symmetry-constrained quantum systems. Exceptional points appear at two levels. In a driven-dissipative Kerr-cat qubit they are defined at the quantum-channel level, mapping a Markovian–non-Markovian phase diagram and enabling switching between regimes. Separately, reservoir engineering in particle-hole Nambu space with a common reservoir is proposed to give exceptional-point sensing a quantum-metrological advantage. For monitored dynamics, trajectory and projected-state ensembles in hybrid measurement-unitary circuits undergo ensemble phase transitions at the measurement-induced point. Correlation diagnostics form a related strand. Gaussian ergotropy of two oscillator detectors probes spatial correlations that distinguish accelerated vacuum from a thermal field. In Z2-symmetric spin models, ground-state squeezing is tied to collective excitation gaps. Less connected items address symmetry-preserving variational circuit design via residual-algebra screening and Hilbert-space-geometry analysis of annealing bottlenecks on Rydberg arrays.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
